1
00:00:39,500 --> 00:00:42,960
Something will work out, I'm sure.

2
00:00:55,920 --> 00:00:59,920
Anyone without a job, come to me.

3
00:00:59,920 --> 00:01:04,520
I don't have one either, but don't worry.

4
00:01:04,520 --> 00:01:18,260
Look, it's on fire.
Akaneru will be fine somehow, eventually.

5
00:01:20,300 --> 00:01:27,460
You understand, right? Got it, got it.
Once you do, shut up and follow me.

6
00:01:40,820 --> 00:01:43,500
Where are we, you old geezer?

7
00:01:45,780 --> 00:01:51,020
Ryo-chan, you bought another cell phone?

8
00:01:52,460 --> 00:01:55,400
Ta-da! This one's got a video camera!

9
00:01:55,400 --> 00:01:58,320
Ryo-chan, you bought another cell phone?

10
00:01:58,680 --> 00:02:00,460
I was just filming a moment ago.

11
00:02:00,460 --> 00:02:03,420
It's the latest model,
you can even send video mail with it!

12
00:02:04,520 --> 00:02:05,900
You can even go shopping with it.

13
00:02:07,480 --> 00:02:09,400
When you hold it up to a barcode reader,

14
00:02:09,400 --> 00:02:12,060
the cost of your purchases is automatically

15
00:02:12,060 --> 00:02:14,100
deducted from your bank account.

16
00:02:14,640 --> 00:02:16,560
So it doubles as a credit card?

17
00:02:17,240 --> 00:02:17,960
That's amazing.

18
00:02:18,560 --> 00:02:21,340
Mine's the new model
that's due out this summer.

19
00:02:21,940 --> 00:02:23,020
With its big screen,

20
00:02:23,020 --> 00:02:25,340
you can read newspaper
articles like this, too.

21
00:02:25,800 --> 00:02:28,360
And you can even watch TV on it.

22
00:02:28,980 --> 00:02:29,700
Wow!

23
00:02:30,080 --> 00:02:32,680
Mine can only make phone calls.

24
00:02:34,520 --> 00:02:39,840
You still have that? I won it four years
ago in a raffle at the local shopping street.

25
00:02:40,260 --> 00:02:45,240
Not that I ever use it. But this thing
was already ancient four years ago.

26
00:02:45,840 --> 00:02:48,600
It can't even send emails Wow, really.

27
00:02:49,120 --> 00:02:51,980
But it does have a clock function, you know.

28
00:02:52,380 --> 00:03:02,700
A phone with no time display? I've never heard of such a thing these days! Right.
Lately, with mobile phones everywhere, nobody wears a wristwatch anymore, do they?

29
00:03:05,520 --> 00:03:07,720
Why don't you make it purely a phone?

30
00:03:07,720 --> 00:03:09,700
Stop it, you two.

31
00:03:11,980 --> 00:03:13,000
It's a phone.

32
00:03:13,000 --> 00:03:13,980
Me too.

33
00:03:13,980 --> 00:03:14,980
Me too.

34
00:03:15,900 --> 00:03:17,120
Hey, gentlemen.

35
00:03:17,680 --> 00:03:19,040
Electrode spark.

36
00:03:19,040 --> 00:03:23,460
When it comes to mobile phones, leave
it to my Super Electronics, my good sir.

37
00:03:23,920 --> 00:03:25,380
This is a videophone, isn't it?

38
00:03:26,040 --> 00:03:26,920
Wow!

39
00:03:26,920 --> 00:03:30,300
But why,
when we're talking about cell phones,

40
00:03:36,020 --> 00:03:38,460
I've heard everything from right here.

41
00:03:38,860 --> 00:03:40,620
You could just come inside.

42
00:03:40,620 --> 00:03:43,520
You just want to show off your phone to everyone,
don't you?

43
00:03:43,520 --> 00:03:47,400
Sure, cell phones are evolving rapidly.

44
00:03:47,400 --> 00:03:50,060
But there's a catch.

45
00:03:50,060 --> 00:03:52,080
What kind of catch?

46
00:03:53,520 --> 00:03:57,520
Like the miniaturization and weight reduction
that cell phone commercials love to showcase.

47
00:03:57,520 --> 00:04:00,560
But if miniaturization and
weight reduction go too far,

48
00:04:00,560 --> 00:04:03,340
the buttons get so small
that it's hard to make calls.

49
00:04:04,320 --> 00:04:09,520
When you hold the speaker to your ear, the mic
is so far away that you end up talking too loudly.

50
00:04:09,520 --> 00:04:10,160
Hello? Hello?

51
00:04:12,100 --> 00:04:13,200
Let's take it with us.

52
00:04:13,200 --> 00:04:19,000
It's so light you don't even
notice when you carry it.

53
00:04:19,560 --> 00:04:22,500
Well, I do still lose them all the time.

54
00:04:22,500 --> 00:04:25,200
So that's why you keep buying new phones

55
00:04:25,660 --> 00:04:28,540
That's what was developed there. This is it.

56
00:04:29,900 --> 00:04:30,700
W-what is that?

57
00:04:30,700 --> 00:04:35,980
Isn't this basically an old black rotary phone?
But it's still a proper mobile phone!

58
00:04:36,580 --> 00:04:42,680
It uses a dial, so you can dial accurately without mistakes, and it's the same size as a home phone,
so

59
00:04:43,300 --> 00:04:49,800
hello, it's me. Keep it in the dedicated
holster and you can talk calmly and confidently.

60
00:04:51,080 --> 00:04:53,960
It has a cord,
so there's no need to worry about losing it.

61
00:04:54,820 --> 00:04:57,420
What do you say?
I'll make it half price, just for now.

62
00:04:57,420 --> 00:04:59,760
Suddenly saying something like that...

63
00:05:00,700 --> 00:05:08,200
Spark, you came all this way to sell phones?
No, I'm in the middle of a test drive for a new car.

64
00:05:11,900 --> 00:05:18,820
This car has an autopilot feature linked to the car navigation.
With autopilot,

65
00:05:23,080 --> 00:05:25,400
just tell the navigation
where you want to go.

66
00:05:25,920 --> 00:05:28,640
Then, Nikoniko Dormitory.

67
00:05:32,000 --> 00:05:33,780
Destination set.

68
00:05:34,260 --> 00:05:37,440
When departing, please place
both hands on the steering wheel.

69
00:05:38,340 --> 00:05:39,400
It's a safety measure.

70
00:05:39,400 --> 00:05:43,040
Gripping the steering wheel firmly
will disengage the autopilot.

71
00:05:44,400 --> 00:05:45,600
Departing now.

72
00:05:46,940 --> 00:05:47,920
Whoa, it moved!

73
00:05:47,920 --> 00:05:50,160
I'll be right back.

74
00:05:54,600 --> 00:05:56,720
Wow, so easy, so easy!

75
00:05:56,720 --> 00:05:59,800
Just resting your hands and it drives on its own,
you know.

76
00:06:00,960 --> 00:06:02,320
Red light.

77
00:06:03,720 --> 00:06:08,600
Don't worry, it automatically recognizes
red lights and applies the brakes.

78
00:06:13,620 --> 00:06:15,620
It keeps a proper following distance

79
00:06:16,020 --> 00:06:18,260
It has collision-prevention sensors.

80
00:06:20,260 --> 00:06:21,720
Is the route changing?

81
00:06:21,720 --> 00:06:24,720
An accident has caused a traffic jam ahead.

82
00:06:25,140 --> 00:06:26,580
Changing the route.

83
00:06:27,980 --> 00:06:29,480
I see, so that's how it works.

84
00:06:30,140 --> 00:06:32,780
It's a feature that conventional
car navigation systems also have,

85
00:06:32,780 --> 00:06:36,700
but until now it took a while for
traffic information to be reflected.

86
00:06:38,600 --> 00:06:39,220
It gets reflected.

87
00:06:45,520 --> 00:06:46,520
It's Saponji.

88
00:06:49,140 --> 00:06:50,260
Hey, Saponji.

89
00:06:51,540 --> 00:06:52,340
Ryotsu.

90
00:06:53,340 --> 00:06:54,740
Where did you get this car?

91
00:06:54,740 --> 00:06:58,400
We're test-driving the new autopilot model.

92
00:06:59,100 --> 00:07:03,320
Under traffic regulations, stopping in
this spot is limited to five minutes.

93
00:07:04,280 --> 00:07:04,640
Amazing.

94
00:07:04,640 --> 00:07:04,920
Amazing!

95
00:07:06,300 --> 00:07:08,740
It's our company's pride and joy, after all!

96
00:07:09,000 --> 00:07:12,400
This navigation voice
sounds just like Saori.

97
00:07:12,400 --> 00:07:15,060
That's what you noticed?

98
00:07:15,060 --> 00:07:18,080
You have arrived at your destination.

99
00:07:18,620 --> 00:07:20,420
Well, guess I'll head
back to the police box.

100
00:07:22,340 --> 00:07:23,460
It's Honda.

101
00:07:24,620 --> 00:07:26,380
It's me! What's up, Honda?

102
00:07:27,440 --> 00:07:29,268
I'm around Tamagawa right now, but

103
00:07:29,318 --> 00:07:31,920
I found a toy that looks pretty interesting.

104
00:07:31,920 --> 00:07:32,700
Really?

105
00:07:34,640 --> 00:07:37,720
Looks like there's a stash
of stuff this boss would love.

106
00:07:38,160 --> 00:07:40,540
Got it. Where exactly are we going?

107
00:07:41,020 --> 00:07:45,060
It's in a spot where the roads
are a bit tangled and hard to find.

108
00:07:45,740 --> 00:07:49,380
In that case,
put your phone in that holder over there.

109
00:07:49,380 --> 00:07:53,700
It pulls the information from the other
party's voice and sets the destination.

110
00:07:53,700 --> 00:07:56,200
Honda's shop name and location are...

111
00:07:56,600 --> 00:07:59,880
Near the toy shop,
by the Takamatsu Tamagawa Bridge.

112
00:08:06,540 --> 00:08:07,300
Alright, it's come up.

113
00:08:07,300 --> 00:08:08,740
Setting the route.

114
00:08:08,740 --> 00:08:11,540
Can I keep this car a little longer?

115
00:08:11,940 --> 00:08:15,300
I'm heading to America on a
business trip on an evening flight.

116
00:08:15,300 --> 00:08:18,180
As long as I reach Narita by then,
it should be fine.

117
00:08:18,180 --> 00:08:20,020
I'll let the company know.

118
00:08:20,680 --> 00:08:21,880
There's plenty of time.

119
00:08:21,880 --> 00:08:23,800
Alright, let's go!

120
00:08:24,360 --> 00:08:25,420
Starting off.

121
00:08:34,640 --> 00:08:40,240
Speaking of which, I haven't had lunch yet. Somewhere near the airport, too...
In times like that, I use the car navigation.

122
00:08:40,240 --> 00:08:44,040
Stop by a fast-food shop along the drive,
will you?

123
00:08:46,100 --> 00:08:47,680
Is this shop all right?

124
00:08:49,400 --> 00:08:50,960
Route changed.

125
00:08:57,980 --> 00:09:00,860
A double cheeseburger set
with hot coffee, please.

126
00:09:01,500 --> 00:09:02,420
What about you?

127
00:09:03,100 --> 00:09:04,200
No, I'm fine.

128
00:09:04,580 --> 00:09:07,980
I'll drive,
so eat slowly in the passenger seat.

129
00:09:07,980 --> 00:09:09,100
Thanks, I owe you one.

130
00:09:09,100 --> 00:09:11,260
No need to get up from your seat.

131
00:09:12,500 --> 00:09:14,040
If you press this switch...

132
00:09:22,400 --> 00:09:24,360
You can switch with just one touch.

133
00:09:24,360 --> 00:09:26,360
It's like a transforming robot!

134
00:09:27,940 --> 00:09:31,480
Sometimes you suddenly feel sleepy,
so that's handy at times like that.

135
00:09:33,360 --> 00:09:36,080
By the way, if you take your
hands off the steering wheel,

136
00:09:36,080 --> 00:09:41,640
and you don't grip it again within five seconds,
the car will pull over.

137
00:09:48,740 --> 00:09:52,680
Like this, it automatically
stops on the shoulder of the road.

138
00:09:54,640 --> 00:09:56,260
Resuming driving.

139
00:10:07,400 --> 00:10:08,720
That was close.

140
00:10:09,100 --> 00:10:10,880
Autopilot has been disengaged.

141
00:10:11,700 --> 00:10:15,280
In situations like this, humans are
better at making split-second decisions.

142
00:10:15,280 --> 00:10:18,400
Coffee got splashed on the car navigation,
but I wonder if it's okay.

143
00:10:19,020 --> 00:10:21,420
It won't break down from
something this small.

144
00:10:23,680 --> 00:10:24,400
Resuming.

145
00:10:35,620 --> 00:10:36,500
Idiot!

146
00:10:38,420 --> 00:10:39,640
That was close.

147
00:10:39,640 --> 00:10:42,720
What a reckless car.

148
00:10:42,720 --> 00:10:44,560
Running a red light like that!

149
00:10:44,560 --> 00:10:47,160
Hey, hey,
it was this car that ran the red light!

150
00:10:47,160 --> 00:10:50,180
Don't talk nonsense.

151
00:10:50,180 --> 00:10:55,040
This car is designed to stop
automatically at red lights.

152
00:10:55,040 --> 00:10:58,000
But it was definitely red,
you saw it too, didn't you?

153
00:10:58,000 --> 00:11:00,940
People can make mistakes when they see things,
you know.

154
00:11:02,840 --> 00:11:05,700
Whoa, hey, this isn't a road!

155
00:11:05,700 --> 00:11:10,340
The car navigation must have
decided this way is a shortcut!

156
00:11:10,340 --> 00:11:11,160
No way, that's ridiculous.

157
00:11:13,560 --> 00:11:16,120
See, we're back on a normal road.

158
00:11:19,600 --> 00:11:21,840
Why is it driving through that yard?

159
00:11:21,840 --> 00:11:24,160
There must be a traffic jam somewhere...

160
00:11:28,520 --> 00:11:31,560
Furano...
I feel like I've heard that name somewhere.

161
00:11:38,760 --> 00:11:40,300
Isn't this the northern land?

162
00:11:40,300 --> 00:11:43,160
It says we're driving in Hokkaido!

163
00:11:43,160 --> 00:11:48,120
Our company's new model,
flying all the way to Hokkaido in one hop!

164
00:11:48,120 --> 00:11:49,580
Just admit it already.

165
00:11:49,580 --> 00:11:51,480
The car navigation is broken,
that's what this is.

166
00:11:53,240 --> 00:11:55,540
Look, we're back in Tokyo.

167
00:12:10,000 --> 00:12:11,580
What a car!

168
00:12:13,240 --> 00:12:14,000
Thank goodness.

169
00:12:14,000 --> 00:12:15,900
It's going to cause a
major accident at this rate.

170
00:12:15,900 --> 00:12:18,100
Quickly turn off the autopilot!

171
00:12:18,560 --> 00:12:20,980
Actually, I've been trying since earlier,

172
00:12:21,720 --> 00:12:23,080
but I can't turn it off.

173
00:12:23,580 --> 00:12:24,360
What?!

174
00:12:25,040 --> 00:12:28,260
Right, if you take your hands off the wheel,
it's supposed to stop automatically, remember?

175
00:12:28,260 --> 00:12:30,020
That's right.

176
00:12:33,280 --> 00:12:34,840
Yes! It's stopping!

177
00:12:35,680 --> 00:12:37,020
It won't stop!

178
00:12:43,680 --> 00:12:45,220
Aaaaaaah!

179
00:12:48,840 --> 00:12:51,120
Hey, stop! Stop, damn it!

180
00:12:51,120 --> 00:12:53,540
Then what do we do?

181
00:12:53,540 --> 00:12:56,140
I told you to stop!

182
00:12:58,780 --> 00:13:00,520
Aaaaaaah!

183
00:13:04,280 --> 00:13:05,400
Aaaaaaah!

184
00:13:05,400 --> 00:13:06,920
Aaaaaaah!

185
00:13:06,920 --> 00:13:07,360
Aaaaaaah!

186
00:13:07,360 --> 00:13:08,380
Aaaaaaah!

187
00:13:16,520 --> 00:13:18,560
Th-the latest car from our company just

188
00:13:19,440 --> 00:13:21,040
Hey, what are you going to do about this?

189
00:13:22,000 --> 00:13:23,420
That's my line!

190
00:13:23,420 --> 00:13:26,020
That car has put me through hell!

191
00:13:26,860 --> 00:13:27,503
Honestly,

192
00:13:27,553 --> 00:13:30,880
at this point, we have no choice but to
call Honda and have them come pick us up.

193
00:13:32,220 --> 00:13:35,680
Oh no, my phone was connected
to the car's navigation!

194
00:13:36,760 --> 00:13:38,400
Hey, give me your phone!

195
00:13:38,400 --> 00:13:41,620
Wait, I have to call for a ride too!

196
00:13:43,340 --> 00:13:45,160
Haaah! Haaah!

197
00:13:45,700 --> 00:13:46,700
Woah!

198
00:13:47,980 --> 00:13:49,740
The phone broke!

199
00:13:49,980 --> 00:13:51,020
Don't yell like that!

200
00:13:53,680 --> 00:13:54,400
What was that?

201
00:13:54,840 --> 00:13:56,400
That's the sound of an email coming in!

202
00:13:58,220 --> 00:14:00,580
No good! I can't read it at all!

203
00:14:02,200 --> 00:14:03,640
Calm down, Spa-kun!

204
00:14:04,080 --> 00:14:06,100
This thing's LCD must just be broken!

205
00:14:06,160 --> 00:14:07,660
Maybe we can make a call!

206
00:14:08,440 --> 00:14:09,640
R-right!

207
00:14:09,640 --> 00:14:12,900
Pull up the phone book function...

208
00:14:15,100 --> 00:14:17,360
Who is it? It's not showing up.

209
00:14:18,220 --> 00:14:22,480
I can't just stand here! Calm down. With a broken LCD,
of course nothing's going to show up!

210
00:14:22,480 --> 00:14:25,800
Voice input... that's right.

211
00:14:27,120 --> 00:14:29,180
The head office... yeah.

212
00:14:29,800 --> 00:14:37,080
Looks like the voice input's broken too Ha what a mess!
Just press the phone number directly.

213
00:14:38,200 --> 00:14:39,840
But I don't remember it.

214
00:14:39,840 --> 00:14:42,000
Damn, this useless thing!

215
00:14:42,000 --> 00:14:43,500
Give it here.

216
00:14:44,180 --> 00:14:46,240
If I call the head office,
someone's bound to be there.

217
00:14:48,120 --> 00:14:49,400
What was the number again?

218
00:14:49,400 --> 00:14:51,780
You don't remember it either, do you?

219
00:14:52,380 --> 00:14:54,900
I used to remember it with a mnemonic.

220
00:14:55,900 --> 00:14:58,920
Come to think of it,
we used to memorize things with mnemonics.

221
00:14:58,920 --> 00:15:00,940
Like "yoroshiku" or "ii kuni."

222
00:15:00,940 --> 00:15:04,740
Ever since cell phones became common,
we just call people by name.

223
00:15:05,580 --> 00:15:07,620
Damn, I can't remember.

224
00:15:08,260 --> 00:15:12,140
Don't you remember anything,
like your home number?

225
00:15:12,660 --> 00:15:15,240
Not to brag,
but I don't remember a single one.

226
00:15:15,800 --> 00:15:18,000
Damn it, somebody tell me!

227
00:15:20,260 --> 00:15:22,640
We can call directory assistance!

228
00:15:22,640 --> 00:15:24,740
I forgot about that option.

229
00:15:27,880 --> 00:15:29,740
What's the number for directory assistance?

230
00:15:31,340 --> 00:15:35,020
I think it's a three-digit
number starting with 1.

231
00:15:35,020 --> 00:15:38,480
Wasn't it 119?

232
00:15:38,480 --> 00:15:40,440
Right, 119 it is.

233
00:15:43,040 --> 00:15:44,920
Yes, this is Fire and Rescue.

234
00:15:46,020 --> 00:15:48,600
Sorry, wrong number.

235
00:15:48,600 --> 00:15:50,580
119 is the fire department.

236
00:15:50,580 --> 00:15:52,080
I see.

237
00:15:53,500 --> 00:15:57,740
Wasn't directory assistance 110?

238
00:15:59,740 --> 00:16:00,800
What about yours

239
00:16:01,140 --> 00:16:02,380
I-I see, that makes sense.

240
00:16:02,740 --> 00:16:06,460
The number, the number, the number...

241
00:16:06,460 --> 00:16:06,960
That's it!

242
00:16:07,820 --> 00:16:10,280
Phones have a redial function, don't they!

243
00:16:10,340 --> 00:16:12,200
Redial!

244
00:16:12,320 --> 00:16:15,060
Since I called the company
when I picked you up earlier,

245
00:16:18,360 --> 00:16:19,900
Yes, this is Fire and Rescue.

246
00:16:19,900 --> 00:16:20,780
Nnnnn!

247
00:16:20,820 --> 00:16:22,720
I-I'm terribly sorry!

248
00:16:23,060 --> 00:16:25,080
We just called the fire station!

249
00:16:25,520 --> 00:16:27,780
If only we'd thought of this sooner!

250
00:16:30,740 --> 00:16:32,040
We have no choice but
to call them one by one.

251
00:16:37,000 --> 00:16:38,760
Yes, this is directory assistance.

252
00:16:39,120 --> 00:16:40,100
Yes!

253
00:16:40,100 --> 00:16:41,660
So it's 104.

254
00:16:41,660 --> 00:16:44,220
I'd like Super Denshi in Tokyo, please.

255
00:16:44,920 --> 00:16:47,400
Certainly. Please hold for a moment.

256
00:16:49,040 --> 00:16:49,560
What?

257
00:16:49,920 --> 00:16:53,140
Memo paper.
Don't you have anything to write with?

258
00:16:54,160 --> 00:16:57,180
Yeah, lately I've been
using my phone as a notepad.

259
00:16:57,660 --> 00:16:59,200
Don't you have any either?

260
00:17:00,840 --> 00:17:03,360
Right, I've been using
my phone's memo function.

261
00:17:03,860 --> 00:17:06,760
I'll read out the number you requested.

262
00:17:07,600 --> 00:17:08,580
Hold on a second.

263
00:17:08,580 --> 00:17:11,060
Quick, find something to write with!

264
00:17:15,080 --> 00:17:18,620
The main number for Super Denshi Co.,
Ltd. is...

265
00:17:25,620 --> 00:17:28,080
This time it's really broken for good.

266
00:17:28,500 --> 00:17:30,880
Whoa, oh no!

267
00:17:38,440 --> 00:17:41,200
Damn it, where's a payphone?

268
00:17:41,200 --> 00:17:44,100
I heard they've been disappearing lately,
you know.

269
00:17:45,320 --> 00:17:47,880
Right, let's use the phone
book function on the cell phone

270
00:17:47,880 --> 00:17:49,580
and call anyone at all,

271
00:17:49,580 --> 00:17:52,540
so they can contact the company for us.
Wouldn't that have been better?

272
00:17:52,960 --> 00:17:54,160
Don't say that after it's broken!

273
00:17:54,340 --> 00:17:56,540
Just do that the next time it breaks!

274
00:17:58,080 --> 00:17:58,440
No way.

275
00:18:00,680 --> 00:18:01,440
Found one!

276
00:18:05,520 --> 00:18:07,720
Look, there's a phone book here too!

277
00:18:07,720 --> 00:18:10,580
Yes! Now we can call the company!

278
00:18:10,900 --> 00:18:11,740
Good for us.

279
00:18:13,500 --> 00:18:13,820
What?

280
00:18:14,600 --> 00:18:15,560
It's a ten-yen coin.

281
00:18:16,100 --> 00:18:19,860
I just bought a new cell phone yesterday,
so I don't have a single yen to waste.

282
00:18:20,180 --> 00:18:22,160
What about you? Don't you have any money?

283
00:18:23,100 --> 00:18:25,440
Unfortunately, I pay for everything by card.

284
00:18:25,440 --> 00:18:27,020
I don't carry cash.

285
00:18:27,440 --> 00:18:28,520
Then what about a phone card?

286
00:18:28,520 --> 00:18:30,960
Why would I carry a phone
card when I have a cell phone?

287
00:18:31,620 --> 00:18:33,520
Then how are we supposed to make a call?

288
00:18:33,520 --> 00:18:34,180
How should I know?

289
00:18:35,160 --> 00:18:36,220
This is a disaster.

290
00:18:36,220 --> 00:18:39,380
We finally made it to a
place with a phone, and yet

291
00:18:40,420 --> 00:18:42,460
And now that I think about it,

292
00:18:42,860 --> 00:18:44,800
even if we do get through to the company,

293
00:18:44,800 --> 00:18:48,400
they won't come pick us up
unless they know where we are.

294
00:18:48,960 --> 00:18:49,920
Don't worry.

295
00:18:49,920 --> 00:18:53,300
The GPS built into my cell
phone will tell them where I am.

296
00:18:53,300 --> 00:18:55,260
But your phone is broken, remember?

297
00:18:57,340 --> 00:18:58,060
So that means

298
00:18:58,420 --> 00:19:01,980
Are you saying we're lost
right in the middle of Tokyo?

299
00:19:02,920 --> 00:19:05,100
W-what do we do?

300
00:19:05,100 --> 00:19:06,340
Calm down, Spark.

301
00:19:07,020 --> 00:19:09,080
How am I supposed to stay
calm in this situation?

302
00:19:09,580 --> 00:19:13,060
On this business trip,
I'm supposed to close a big deal.

303
00:19:13,060 --> 00:19:16,020
If I stand them up,
we'll lose hundreds of billions.

304
00:19:17,160 --> 00:19:22,120
Come to think of it, that email from
earlier might have been something important.

305
00:19:22,700 --> 00:19:23,520
It's bugging me.

306
00:19:23,520 --> 00:19:26,620
Stop it, I said stop it.
Come on, just a little more.

307
00:19:28,840 --> 00:19:29,240
That's it!

308
00:19:31,260 --> 00:19:34,868
If we walk like this,
keeping one hand on one wall,

309
00:19:34,918 --> 00:19:37,080
we're sure to reach our destination.

310
00:19:48,140 --> 00:19:50,080
I think we passed through here before.

311
00:19:50,200 --> 00:19:51,320
This is the place from earlier, isn't it.

312
00:19:52,480 --> 00:19:54,940
No way! How did we end up back here?

313
00:19:54,940 --> 00:19:58,340
Wasn't that supposed to be the
way to get out of being lost?

314
00:19:58,340 --> 00:20:00,780
Haaaaah...

315
00:20:00,780 --> 00:20:02,560
Uuuuugh...

316
00:20:02,560 --> 00:20:05,080
Right, the Big Dipper!

317
00:20:05,080 --> 00:20:07,620
We find the North Star from the Big Dipper.

318
00:20:07,620 --> 00:20:10,000
That way we'll know which
direction we came from.

319
00:20:10,000 --> 00:20:12,300
Come on, the Big Dipper...

320
00:20:13,040 --> 00:20:15,320
You can't see constellations in the daytime,
you idiot.

321
00:20:16,340 --> 00:20:17,820
Right, the wristwatch!

322
00:20:18,420 --> 00:20:20,880
Point the hour hand of
the wristwatch at the sun.

323
00:20:20,880 --> 00:20:26,320
The midpoint between the hour hand and the 12
o'clock mark on the dial should point south.

324
00:20:27,480 --> 00:20:29,040
Come on, the wristwatch...

325
00:20:29,040 --> 00:20:31,800
Unfortunately, I don't carry a wristwatch.

326
00:20:32,180 --> 00:20:34,160
But my phone has a clock on it.

327
00:20:34,700 --> 00:20:37,080
So we end up back at the phone after all.

328
00:20:39,040 --> 00:20:42,420
Why did my phone have to break?

329
00:20:43,080 --> 00:20:45,440
Hey, calm down, Spark!

330
00:20:48,940 --> 00:20:51,820
If it's a phone call,
shall I make it for you?

331
00:20:54,720 --> 00:20:57,340
It's me, Spark, the president.

332
00:20:57,820 --> 00:20:59,800
Come pick us up at the
address I'm about to give you.

333
00:21:01,080 --> 00:21:05,400
Well, it's been about thirty years
since anyone came to borrow a phone.

334
00:21:05,780 --> 00:21:07,600
People used to come all the time, though.

335
00:21:08,360 --> 00:21:12,240
Yeah, back then quite a few
houses didn't have a phone.

336
00:21:13,740 --> 00:21:16,720
Once we're in touch,
someone will be here right away.

337
00:21:16,720 --> 00:21:17,580
I see.

338
00:21:18,180 --> 00:21:18,600
Please, go ahead.

339
00:21:18,600 --> 00:21:20,680
No, I'm fine.

340
00:21:20,680 --> 00:21:23,740
Don't be shy, it's a kind gesture!

341
00:21:24,440 --> 00:21:26,280
Yes, thank you. I'll accept.

342
00:21:35,700 --> 00:21:38,080
Back in the day,
a lot of houses were like this.

343
00:21:40,080 --> 00:21:42,240
Nothing like your place

344
00:21:42,240 --> 00:21:43,860
Mind your own business.

345
00:21:43,860 --> 00:21:46,500
Do you old-timers even have cell phones?

346
00:21:46,920 --> 00:21:47,780
No way.

347
00:21:48,600 --> 00:21:51,300
But when you need to make a call outside,

348
00:21:51,840 --> 00:21:54,220
for those urgent calls
you absolutely have to make,

349
00:21:54,220 --> 00:21:56,180
they don't come up all that often, right?

350
00:21:56,180 --> 00:21:57,320
Th-that's...

351
00:21:58,140 --> 00:22:01,220
In a pinch,
you can just borrow someone's phone.

352
00:22:01,220 --> 00:22:03,220
That's what you two did too, didn't you?

353
00:22:04,740 --> 00:22:07,720
If you get lost, you can just ask someone.

354
00:22:10,480 --> 00:22:11,000
Hey.

355
00:22:13,880 --> 00:22:20,080
Maybe we've been so busy being manipulated by our
handy gadgets that we've let them run our lives.

356
00:22:21,800 --> 00:22:24,120
Being manipulated by our tools.

357
00:22:27,300 --> 00:22:31,140
Thanks for looking after us.
It was nothing much.

358
00:22:31,720 --> 00:22:35,620
When people are in trouble,
we all help each other, right?

359
00:22:35,620 --> 00:22:39,200
Honda, sorry, but can you lend me ten yen?

360
00:22:39,200 --> 00:22:42,760
I have some, but it's fine, right?

361
00:22:44,960 --> 00:22:49,160
When you borrow a phone,
doing this is simply good manners.

362
00:22:50,380 --> 00:22:51,260
Right?

363
00:22:51,260 --> 00:22:57,820
Uh-huh, uh-huh, uh-huh, got it. I'll wait.

364
00:22:58,920 --> 00:23:00,820
Who was that from, senpai?

365
00:23:00,820 --> 00:23:05,540
It was Super Denshi's electrode spark.
They said someone's coming here soon.

366
00:23:05,960 --> 00:23:09,420
Speaking of which, I heard the
contract in America went well too.

367
00:23:09,840 --> 00:23:11,880
We owe all of it to that elderly couple.

368
00:23:11,880 --> 00:23:14,160
They're the ones who
lent us the phone, right?

369
00:23:14,960 --> 00:23:16,760
So what brings you here today?

370
00:23:17,360 --> 00:23:20,460
I hear you've developed some new product.

371
00:23:23,140 --> 00:23:24,740
Hey, sorry to keep you waiting.

372
00:23:24,740 --> 00:23:28,440
You're here. So what kind of
product did you develop this time?

373
00:23:29,020 --> 00:23:33,220
Your words about being manipulated by
tools really struck a chord with me.

374
00:23:33,960 --> 00:23:37,080
So instead of being manipulated by tools,

375
00:23:38,940 --> 00:23:41,540
I came up with tools that dance.

376
00:23:45,260 --> 00:23:47,140
How about that? Amazing, right?

377
00:23:48,920 --> 00:23:51,120
You don't understand a thing!

378
00:23:51,820 --> 00:23:53,600
What, tools that dance?

379
00:23:53,600 --> 00:23:55,680
Don't talk such nonsense.

380
00:23:55,680 --> 00:23:59,580
You went and wasted someone's goodwill.

381
00:24:11,660 --> 00:24:12,880
I wonder why.

382
00:24:12,880 --> 00:24:14,180
I wonder why.

383
00:24:14,180 --> 00:24:14,780
I wonder why.

384
00:24:14,780 --> 00:24:15,460
I wonder why.

385
00:24:15,460 --> 00:24:16,160
I wonder why.

386
00:24:17,180 --> 00:24:18,480
I wonder why.

387
00:24:19,760 --> 00:24:23,900
Ryo-san's eyebrows look like black seagulls.

388
00:24:23,900 --> 00:24:24,920
I wonder why.

389
00:24:26,140 --> 00:24:30,340
He can run really fast even in sandals.

390
00:24:30,340 --> 00:24:31,420
I wonder why.

391
00:24:33,760 --> 00:24:38,220
Ryo-san always has his sleeves rolled up.

392
00:24:38,220 --> 00:24:39,340
I wonder why.

393
00:24:40,320 --> 00:24:44,560
He's a total mess, yet everyone adores him.

394
00:24:44,560 --> 00:24:45,640
I wonder why.

395
00:24:48,460 --> 00:24:51,560
Not that it matters, but

396
00:24:52,600 --> 00:24:58,300
why is this anime's title so long?

397
00:24:58,880 --> 00:25:01,100
This is Kameari Kōen-mae Police Box,
Katsushika Ward

398
00:25:01,100 --> 00:25:02,800
I wonder why

399
00:25:02,800 --> 00:25:05,280
I wonder why

400
00:25:06,940 --> 00:25:08,580
I wonder why

401
00:25:08,580 --> 00:25:09,680
I wonder why

402
00:25:09,680 --> 00:25:11,040
I wonder why

403
00:25:11,040 --> 00:25:11,780
I wonder why

404
00:25:12,600 --> 00:25:13,980
I wonder why

405
00:25:13,980 --> 00:25:15,320
I wonder why

406
00:25:18,140 --> 00:25:19,300
I wonder why

407
00:25:48,520 --> 00:25:50,840
Over there is Kameary

408
00:25:51,840 --> 00:25:54,720
Well, surely it's Kameary

409
00:25:55,440 --> 00:25:59,200
Everyone, come on over to our town

410
00:26:00,300 --> 00:26:00,820
Thanks

